'use strict';
const {course}=require('./data-helper');
const specs=[
{id:'dsm-center',comps:['m1.c1'],title:'Select a summary of center that matches the question',intro:'An operations manager asks what a typical support resolution takes. The arithmetic mean, median and mode summarize different properties of the same data; choosing one should follow the decision, not a preferred headline.',sections:[['Mean and median have different interpretations','The arithmetic mean is total divided by count and is useful when totals matter: mean handling time multiplied by the number of comparable cases reconstructs total handling time. The median is the middle of ordered values, averaging the two central observations for an even count. Half the observations lie at or below a median and half at or above it, subject to ties.','Extreme values pull the mean because every magnitude contributes to the total. The median is less sensitive to a few large values, but it does not describe workload totals or the severity of long waits. Reporting both can expose a mismatch between an ordinary case and resource burden.'],['Mode and the scale of measurement','The mode is the most frequent observed value or category. A dataset can have multiple modes; a continuous measure with high precision may have no useful repeated value. Python multimode returns every tied most-frequent value, while a single-mode function may select one according to its documented convention.','For a product-size category, a mode can inform stock choices. Averaging category labels or numerical customer identifiers has no meaningful interpretation. When quantities have unequal exposure or importance, determine whether a weighted mean is justified and state those weights rather than silently counting every row equally.']],problem:'Compare center summaries for a small synthetic resolution-time sample.',steps:['Order the times and inspect the extreme value.','Compute mean and median under their definitions.','Find every tied most-frequent time.'],code:'from statistics import mean,median,multimode\ntimes=[2,2,3,3,40]\nprint(mean(times))\nprint(median(times))\nprint(multimode(times))',expect:'10\n3\n[2, 3]',limit:'These summaries describe five fixture cases. They do not establish representative sampling or identify a universally best definition of typical.',mistakes:['A median cannot be multiplied by case count to recover total work in general.','A single reported mode can hide ties or be unhelpful for high-precision continuous values.'],takeaways:['Choose a center measure from its operational meaning.','Use multiple complementary summaries when tails matter.'],practice:[
['Which summary multiplied by count recovers the total?','Arithmetic mean','Median','Mode','Maximum','By definition the mean equals sum divided by count, so multiplying restores that sum.'],
['For [2,2,3,3,40], which value is the median?','3','10','2','40','The sorted sample has five observations and the third is three.'],
['What does multimode return for [2,2,3,3,40]?','Both 2 and 3','Only 40','The mean 10','No values','Two and three each appear twice, tying for the highest frequency.'],
['A staffing planner needs total workload from case counts. Which center measure directly supports this?','Mean time per case','Only median time','Only most common time','Only shortest time','The arithmetic mean preserves the relation between average time, count and total resource use.'],
['A few very long waits inflate the mean. Which summary can describe the middle case alongside it?','The median','The sum only','The largest value','The row identifier','The median locates the ordered middle and is less sensitive to the magnitude of a few extreme waits.'],
['A shop’s size labels are S,M,L. Which center concept may be meaningful?','Most frequent category','Average of encoded ids','Product of category names','Squared category label','Mode uses category frequency without assuming an artificial numerical distance between labels.'],
['Two groups have unequal case counts. Which combined mean is justified for all cases?','Count-weighted group means','Always an equal mean of means','Only the larger mean','Only the smaller mean','Weighting by group counts reproduces the overall total divided by the overall count.']
],formal:[
['Which statistic uses every magnitude directly in its sum?','Arithmetic mean','Median','Mode','Minimum','The mean adds all values, so unusually large or small magnitudes affect it directly.'],
['For ordered [1,4,6,9], what is the median?','5','4','6','20','With an even count, average the two middle observations: (4+6)/2=5.'],
['Can a categorical sample have several modes?','Yes, tied highest frequencies','No, exactly one always','Only for numerical values','Only with missing rows','Multiple categories can share the largest count, producing multiple modes.'],
['A delivery report calls mean=25 minutes a typical customer experience while most cases take five minutes. What should be added?','Median and tail context','Only a larger title','Only a rounded mean','Only the shortest delivery','The mean can be dominated by a long tail; complementary summaries describe the central and tail experience.'],
['A team multiplies median time by cases to estimate exact total work. What is wrong?','Median does not preserve totals','Medians cannot have units','Counts must be negative','Total work has no meaning','Only the arithmetic mean has the defining sum/count relationship needed to reconstruct the total.'],
['A retailer averages product codes to decide the usual item. What is inappropriate?','Arithmetic on identifiers','Counting repeated products','Finding popular categories','Recording item names','Identifier numbers label products; their arithmetic midpoint need not identify any meaningful typical product.'],
['A sample of continuously measured times has no repeated values. What limits the mode?','It may not summarize usefully','The mean is undefined','The median is forbidden','The sample has no order','With unique or nearly unique continuous values, frequency modes offer little description of central tendency.'],
['One branch handles 10 cases at mean 4 minutes and another 90 at mean 8. What is the overall mean?','7.6 minutes','6 minutes','12 minutes','4.4 minutes','The combined total is 10×4+90×8=760 minutes across 100 cases.'],
['A manager removes the largest legitimate resolution to lower the average. What needs justification?','The exclusion policy','Only chart label length','Only the sort direction','Only output formatting','Valid cases should not be excluded merely to improve a preferred summary; exclusions change the target population.'],
['Two samples share a median but one has extreme workload costs. Which conclusion follows?','Equal medians do not mean equal workload','The totals must be identical','Every mean must match','The distributions are identical','A median constrains the ordered middle, not the total or the full distribution of magnitudes.']
]},
{id:'dsm-skew',comps:['m1.c2'],title:'Investigate skew and outliers before changing records',intro:'Transaction values are often right-skewed: many small amounts and a few large ones. A large value may be an error, a rare valid sale or the very case the business needs to understand.',sections:[['Describe the tail rather than labeling every extreme an error','Right skew means a distribution has a relatively long right tail; left skew has a longer left tail. The mean is often pulled toward a heavy tail, but simple mean/median ordering is a heuristic rather than a complete distribution test. Inspect a plot, counts and domain context.','An outlier is unusual relative to a stated model or comparison group. It is not automatically inaccurate. A ten-day shipment delay may indicate an operational incident; removing it erases the relevant failure. An impossible negative mass, in contrast, can violate a known domain constraint.'],['Use flags to organize investigation','An IQR fence flags values below Q1−1.5×IQR or above Q3+1.5×IQR under a conventional descriptive rule. Quartile methods differ, especially in small samples, so record the method. Fences are prompts for review, not proof of fraud or a universal deletion rule.','Check source units, duplicates, context and collection errors. If a legitimate tail is retained, choose summaries or models that acknowledge it. If a correction/exclusion is justified, preserve raw evidence and document its reason, impact and policy.']],problem:'Flag unusual values using explicitly supplied quartiles.',steps:['Declare Q1 and Q3 instead of hiding a quantile convention.','Calculate the IQR fences.','Return flagged observations for review without deleting them.'],code:'values=[10,11,12,13,14,80]\nq1,q3=11,14\niqr=q3-q1\nlow,high=q1-1.5*iqr,q3+1.5*iqr\nprint(low,high)\nprint([v for v in values if v<low or v>high])\nprint(len(values))',expect:'6.5 18.5\n[80]\n6',limit:'The quartiles are supplied for this deterministic illustration. The fence flags 80 under this rule but does not establish that the value is erroneous.',mistakes:['An unusual observation can be a valid high-impact event.','Different quartile conventions can change small-sample flags.'],takeaways:['Separate unusual, impossible and incorrect.','Investigate and document changes instead of deleting automatically.'],practice:[
['What characterizes a right-skewed distribution?','A longer right tail','Every value is positive','Every case is an error','A symmetric shape','Skew concerns the asymmetry of tails, not merely the sign of the values.'],
['Does an outlier flag prove a record is wrong?','No','Yes universally','Only if above the mean','Only if it is numeric','A flag marks unusual behavior under a chosen rule; source/domain review determines whether it is erroneous.'],
['What is IQR?','Q3−Q1','Maximum−minimum','Mean−median','Q1+Q3','The interquartile range measures the spread of the middle half using the chosen quartiles.'],
['A shipment took ten days during a real outage. What should a delay analysis do?','Retain/investigate the valid event','Delete it to improve averages','Replace it with zero','Assume the timestamp is false','A legitimate extreme can be central evidence of operational harm and should not be erased for appearance.'],
['A mass field contains -7kg under a nonnegative-mass contract. What is indicated?','A domain-rule violation','A typical right tail','A guaranteed valid package','A mode calculation','Negative physical mass violates the stated domain constraint and needs source handling rather than a generic tail interpretation.'],
['Two tools flag different IQR outliers in a tiny sample. What should be checked?','Their quantile conventions','Only their logos','Only file compression','Only row-id spelling','Different interpolation/quartile definitions can produce different small-sample boundaries.'],
['A team excludes extremes only when the result improves. What is the risk?','Outcome-driven selection','Guaranteed unbiased cleaning','Exact population preservation','Automatic uncertainty reduction','Selective exclusions can bias summaries and hide important cases; a defensible policy must precede the desired outcome.']
],formal:[
['Which observation is automatically inaccurate merely because it is far from the mean?','None without further evidence','Every large value','Every negative value','Every upper-quartile value','Distance from a center alone does not establish a measurement or collection error.'],
['Which expression is a conventional upper IQR fence?','Q3+1.5×IQR','Q1−Q3','Mean+median','Q1/1.5','The common descriptive fence extends 1.5 interquartile ranges above Q3.'],
['What should accompany an outlier rule?','Its comparison context','A preferred chart color','Only a shorter filename','A guarantee of fraud','Unusualness depends on the relevant population, scale, model and domain, not a value in isolation.'],
['A retail bulk purchase is ten times the usual basket. What is a sound first step?','Check its business/source context','Delete it immediately','Convert it to the median','Call it fraud automatically','Bulk purchases may be valid; investigation should establish whether the case fits the intended population and source records.'],
['A bank flags every large transfer as an error. Which distinction is missing?','Unusual versus incorrect','Mean versus sum only','CSV versus JSON only','Time versus currency only','A large valid transfer can be important business evidence even if uncommon.'],
['A support-time distribution has a long upper tail. Which report is more informative?','Center plus tail summaries','Only a binary good badge','Only the smallest case','Only the row count','Tail percentiles/extremes alongside appropriate centers show rare but consequential delays.'],
['An analyst silently caps amounts at an arbitrary threshold. What should be documented?','The capping rule and impact','Only spreadsheet version','Only cell alignment','Only the column color','Capping changes observed magnitudes and can alter totals, relationships and conclusions.'],
['A sensor’s unit accidentally changes from Celsius to Fahrenheit. Why might outliers appear?','A measurement-scale change','A proven real-world incident','Automatic random sampling','An identical distribution','Unit changes can produce apparent extremes without a genuine change in the underlying physical process.'],
['A descriptive fence flags an observation. Which conclusion remains unsupported?','That the event is fraudulent','That the rule flagged it','That the value was compared','That a boundary was defined','Statistical unusualness is not evidence sufficient to identify intent or fraud.'],
['A legitimate tail dominates financial loss. Why can median-only reporting be inadequate?','It can hide costly extremes','It always overstates loss','It reconstructs total loss exactly','It forces a normal shape','The median describes the middle observation, not the magnitude or operational burden of rare losses.']
]},
{id:'dsm-spread',comps:['m1.c1','m1.c2'],title:'Report variation and percentiles with explicit conventions',intro:'Two service centers can share the same average while giving customers very different experiences. Spread and percentiles describe variation that a center statistic cannot capture.',sections:[['Match the spread measure to the purpose','Range is maximum minus minimum and is sensitive to the most extreme observations. Population variance averages squared deviations from the population mean; sample variance commonly divides by n−1 to estimate population variance under independent sampling. Standard deviation is the square root of variance and returns to the original measurement unit.','IQR describes middle-half spread and is less sensitive to distant tail magnitudes. No single spread statistic replaces inspection of shape and data quality. A small standard deviation does not establish accuracy if every measurement has a common bias.'],['Percentiles require an estimator and a population','A percentile marks a location in an ordered distribution. In finite samples, methods may interpolate or select ranks differently; record the convention when thresholds matter. A 95th-percentile duration is not a 95% confidence interval for the mean.','Weighted observations, repeated users and censoring can change which percentile calculation answers the question. The fixture compares a constant sample with an equally centered variable sample, then computes an explicitly named inclusive quantile method. It describes the supplied cases, not the precision of population-tail estimates.']],problem:'Compare equal-center datasets and inspect a documented quartile estimator.',steps:['Compute means for two small samples.','Compare population standard deviations.','Calculate quartiles with the stated inclusive method.'],code:'from statistics import mean,pstdev,quantiles\na,b=[5,5,5,5],[1,3,7,9]\nprint(mean(a),mean(b))\nprint(pstdev(a),round(pstdev(b),6))\nprint(quantiles(b,n=4,method="inclusive"))',expect:'5 5\n0.0 3.162278\n[2.5, 5.0, 7.5]',limit:'The sample summaries and chosen interpolation rule are reproducible. They do not estimate tail uncertainty or prove the underlying populations have the same mean.',mistakes:['Variance and standard deviation have different units.','A tail percentile is not an interval estimate for a population parameter.'],takeaways:['Center and spread answer complementary questions.','Record variance and quantile conventions.'],practice:[
['What units does variance have?','Squared measurement units','The original units always','No units always','Record identifiers','Squaring deviations squares the unit; standard deviation takes the root back to the original unit.'],
['What is the range?','Maximum minus minimum','Q3 minus Q1 always','Mean minus median','Sum divided by count','Range spans the two observed extremes, unlike the IQR’s middle-half spread.'],
['Can equal means coexist with different spread?','Yes','No, mean fixes every value','Only for negative data','Only with missing values','Many distributions and samples have the same center but very different variation.'],
['Two queues average five minutes, but one varies from one to nine. What should be compared?','Their variability','Only matching means','Only queue names','Only report dates','A center alone does not describe consistency or the chance of long waits.'],
['A chart labels duration variance as minutes rather than minutes squared. What is wrong?','The unit label','The existence of a mean','The sample count','The idea of duration','Variance uses squared deviations; its unit is squared time, unlike standard deviation.'],
['A p95 latency is confused with a 95% mean confidence interval. What differs?','Outcome tail versus estimate uncertainty','Only percentage notation','Only decimal places','Nothing statistical','A percentile describes outcome location; a confidence interval describes an estimator’s uncertainty under a procedure.'],
['Two reports use different percentile interpolation methods. What is needed for comparison?','A shared estimator convention','Only a shared logo','Only equal filenames','Only a larger sample id','Finite-sample percentiles can vary by method, especially near sparse tails or thresholds.']
],formal:[
['Which measure returns to the original unit after variance?','Standard deviation','Squared error','Covariance with itself','Variance squared','Taking the square root of squared-unit variance yields standard deviation in the original unit.'],
['For [5,5,5], what is population standard deviation?','0','5','15','25','Every deviation from the mean is zero, so variance and standard deviation are zero.'],
['What does IQR emphasize?','The middle half’s spread','Only the largest observation','Only the sample average','The total across all values','Q3−Q1 measures the interval between the lower and upper quartiles.'],
['A hospital reports only average waiting time although a few waits are extreme. What complements it?','Tail and spread measures','Only another average label','Only the smallest wait','Only patient-id length','Variation and upper-tail summaries reveal service experiences hidden by a mean alone.'],
['A sample variance divides by n−1. What should the documentation state?','The sample-estimator convention','That every row is a population','That units disappear','That all errors are removed','The denominator distinguishes an inferential sample convention from a population descriptive variance.'],
['A business changes from population to sample standard deviation in a trend. What needs disclosure?','The estimator change','Only the chart orientation','Only the report author','Only table width','Different denominators can change results even on the same observations, affecting comparability.'],
['A sensor is consistently five units too high but has low spread. What is not established?','Measurement accuracy','Low observed variation','A repeated value pattern','A numerical center','Precision/low spread does not rule out a common systematic bias.'],
['A small dataset’s p99 is presented as a precise population guarantee. What is missing?','Tail-estimation uncertainty','Only a larger font','Only an integer data type','Only a sorted export','Extreme percentiles from few observations are uncertain and depend strongly on sampling and estimator choices.'],
['A dashboard combines spread from two groups by averaging their standard deviations. What needs checking?','Between-group and weighting effects','Only whether values are positive','Only the group label length','Only spreadsheet formatting','Overall variance includes within-group and between-group differences; standard deviations do not generally combine by a simple mean.'],
['A team reports the same median and IQR for two samples. Can their upper-tail losses still differ?','Yes, those summaries do not fix tails','No, the samples must match','Only if one mean is missing','Only with categorical values','Middle quantiles leave substantial freedom in the extreme observations and total loss.']
]},
{id:'dsm-growth',comps:['m2.c1'],title:'Calculate growth, conversion and changes on the right scale',intro:'A business can grow revenue while its conversion rate falls because the number and mix of visitors changed. Separate levels, rates, absolute differences and relative changes before explaining performance.',sections:[['Growth has a baseline and a time basis','For a nonzero baseline B and new value N, relative change is (N−B)/B. A change from 100 to 120 is +20%, while returning from 120 to 100 is about −16.7%; the denominators differ. Zero or negative baselines need a policy because an ordinary growth percentage can be undefined or misleading.','Compare equivalent periods or explicitly normalize exposure. Month-over-month and year-over-year changes answer different questions about seasonality. Compound growth multiplies period factors: +10% followed by −10% leaves 99% of the original, not 100%. A CAGR summarizes endpoints over elapsed intervals and does not describe every intervening fluctuation.'],['Rates and volumes move independently','A conversion rate needs the eligible entity/event definition established in Data and Business Analysis Fundamentals. When rate changes are reported, distinguish percentage points from relative uplift and show numerator/denominator volumes. A growing denominator can produce more conversions even as the rate falls.','The worked example uses exact fractions for two synthetic periods. It checks volume, conversion and compounding calculations, not the causal explanation for changing demand or whether the compared populations are alike.']],problem:'Separate conversion-rate movement, conversion counts and compounded changes.',steps:['Calculate each period’s rate from aligned counts.','Compare absolute rate change with count growth.','Multiply sequential growth factors.'],code:'from fractions import Fraction\na,b=Fraction(10,100),Fraction(18,200)\nprint(a,b)\nprint(b-a)\nprint(Fraction(18-10,10))\nprint(Fraction(110,100)*Fraction(90,100))',expect:'1/10 9/100\n-1/100\n4/5\n99/100',limit:'Counts rose while conversion fell in these fixtures. No causal driver or seasonal adjustment is established by the arithmetic.',mistakes:['A percentage change uses a baseline; reversing the direction changes that baseline.','Sequential percentage changes should be compounded, not simply added.'],takeaways:['Report counts and rates together.','State baseline, exposure and comparison period.'],practice:[
['A total rises from 100 to 120. What is relative growth?','20%','120%','16.7%','2%','The increase twenty divided by the original one hundred is twenty percent.'],
['A total returns from 120 to 100. Is the decline exactly 20%?','No','Yes by symmetry','Only if rounded to zero','Only if counts are integers','The decrease twenty is divided by 120, giving about sixteen-point-seven percent.'],
['What is the ordinary relative-growth calculation when baseline is zero?','Undefined without a policy','Always zero','Always one hundred percent','Equal to the new total percent','Division by zero is undefined; report an appropriate absolute change or explicit alternative convention.'],
['Conversions rise from 10/100 to 18/200. What happened?','More conversions, lower rate','Fewer conversions, higher rate','Both count and rate fell','Both count and rate rose','The count rises from ten to eighteen while the rate falls from ten to nine percent.'],
['A price rises 10% then falls 10%. What is its endpoint relative to the start?','99%','100%','90%','110%','Compounded factors are 1.1×0.9=0.99.'],
['One report compares a 31-day month with a 28-day month using totals only. What must be considered?','Exposure duration','Only the month labels','Only cell formatting','Only product ordering','Different operating exposure can affect totals independently of underlying daily performance.'],
['A growth headline omits a small starting count. Why show that count?','To reveal the baseline scale','To prove causation','To remove uncertainty','To force equal rates','Large relative growth from tiny baselines can represent small absolute changes and unstable estimates.']
],formal:[
['Which formula gives relative change from positive B to N?','(N−B)/B','N−B only','B/N always','N+B','The baseline-normalized difference measures proportional change from the original value.'],
['What combines sequential proportional changes?','Product of growth factors','Sum of percentages always','Mean of endpoint labels','Maximum of period totals','Multiply factors such as 1+g for each period to obtain the compounded endpoint ratio.'],
['What does CAGR summarize?','An endpoint-equivalent annual factor','Each actual annual fluctuation','A causal yearly intervention','A guaranteed future return','CAGR is a constant compound rate matching start and end over the specified elapsed intervals.'],
['A store reports 200% growth from one sale to three. What should also be visible?','The absolute counts','Only a bigger percent sign','Only the chart color','Only the store number','The relative change is correct but the tiny baseline and absolute increase matter for interpretation.'],
['A retailer’s traffic doubles while conversion falls slightly. Can completed orders rise?','Yes, volume can offset rate loss','No, any rate drop lowers counts','Only if revenue is negative','Only if users are renamed','Count equals eligible volume times rate under the definition, so a larger denominator can outweigh a lower rate.'],
['A team adds +20% and -20% and claims no net change. What is the net factor?','0.96','1.00','1.20','0.80','The sequential product 1.2×0.8 is 0.96, a four-percent decline.'],
['A report uses profit growth from a negative baseline as a simple success percentage. What is needed?','An explicit interpretation policy','Only more decimal places','Only a wider graph','Only a larger database type','Negative baselines can make ordinary percentage-growth signs unintuitive; absolute amounts and conventions need disclosure.'],
['A subscription dashboard compares month-over-month and year-over-year growth interchangeably. What differs?','The comparison reference','Only decimal notation','Only report orientation','Nothing quantitative','The periods answer different questions and interact differently with seasonality and recent trends.'],
['An analyst computes endpoint growth over five dates as five elapsed intervals. What should be checked?','The interval count','Only date display width','Only filename order','Only the largest total','Five consecutive annual observations normally span four annual intervals; elapsed time governs compounding.'],
['A conversion increase accompanies an acquisition-channel mix change. What cannot be inferred from the aggregate alone?','A causal product improvement','The aggregate rates','That channel mix changed','The observed conversion count','Population composition and other changes can explain aggregate movement without a product intervention effect.']
]},
{id:'dsm-margin',comps:['m2.c2'],title:'Reconcile revenue, refunds, costs and margin definitions',intro:'Two finance dashboards can disagree while both sum their rows correctly. One may use gross orders, another settled revenue net of refunds. A metric dictionary must distinguish business states and cost conventions.',sections:[['Separate the stages and amounts','Gross booked order value, invoiced revenue, recognized revenue and collected cash refer to different concepts. Which amount is appropriate depends on the business/accounting policy and decision. Refunds, discounts, cancellations, tax and shipping may be included or excluded under explicit rules; do not invent a universal revenue policy.','Maintain signed transactions or reviewed adjustment rules with stable references and periods. A refund today for a prior order can affect a current-period cash report differently from an order-cohort performance report. Reconciliation should explain these timing and scope differences rather than force totals to match by arbitrary edits.'],['Margin is not markup','Under a stated policy, gross profit equals net revenue minus cost of goods sold. Gross margin divides gross profit by net revenue, while markup divides gross profit by cost. A product selling for 100 with cost 60 has margin 40% and markup about 66.7%. A zero denominator requires an explicit undefined result.','The worked fixture declares net revenue as gross receipts minus refunds and excludes tax/shipping entirely. Costs are supplied for the same cohort. Decimal arithmetic preserves the specified cent amounts but cannot validate accounting recognition, currency conversion or source completeness.']],problem:'Compute a declared net-revenue margin and distinguish markup.',steps:['Apply the fixture’s explicit refund rule.','Subtract matched costs from net revenue.','Divide profit by revenue and by cost separately.'],code:'from decimal import Decimal\ngross,refunds,cost=map(Decimal,["100.00","10.00","54.00"])\nnet=gross-refunds\nprofit=net-cost\nprint(net,profit)\nprint(profit/net)\nprint((profit/cost).quantize(Decimal("0.0001")))',expect:'90.00 36.00\n0.4\n0.6667',limit:'This verifies the declared toy metric policy and decimal arithmetic. Real accounting recognition, tax, currencies and refund-period treatment require approved organizational rules.',mistakes:['Gross margin and markup use different denominators.','A settled payment and recognized revenue are not automatically the same business measure.'],takeaways:['Version the actual financial metric rules.','Reconcile scope, state, cohort and timing before comparing totals.'],practice:[
['Which denominator defines gross margin under the stated policy?','Net revenue','Cost of goods sold','Number of orders','All website visits','Gross margin is gross profit divided by net revenue; markup uses cost instead.'],
['For revenue 100 and cost 60, what is gross profit?','40','60','100','160','Subtract the matched cost of goods sold from the stated revenue.'],
['Does decimal arithmetic alone validate a revenue definition?','No','Yes universally','Only for positive amounts','Only if refunds exist','Numerical representation cannot establish the correct accounting/business scope or completeness.'],
['A refund occurs this month for last month’s order. Why might two reports differ?','Their timing/cohort policy','Only their chart colors','Only their row sort order','Only their title length','A transaction-period cash view and an order-cohort view can assign the adjustment to different periods.'],
['A system includes tax in one revenue metric and excludes it in another. What must be reconciled?','The metric scope','Only database column width','Only export format','Only record identifiers','Tax inclusion changes what the amount means, even if both sums are arithmetically correct.'],
['A product has net revenue zero. What is its ordinary margin ratio?','Undefined without a policy','Always zero percent','Always one hundred percent','Equal to its cost','The denominator is zero, so the ratio needs an explicit undefined/alternative reporting rule.'],
['Two dashboards disagree on cancellations. What should happen before comparing performance?','Align documented status rules','Select the larger total','Average the totals immediately','Delete canceled rows silently','Inclusion/exclusion policies determine population and metric meaning and must be agreed or explained.']
],formal:[
['What distinguishes margin from markup?','Their denominators','Their currency symbol only','Their record ordering only','Nothing mathematically','Margin divides profit by revenue; markup divides profit by cost.'],
['Under net=revenue−refunds, what is net for 200 revenue and 30 refunds?','170','230','200','30','The declared policy subtracts refunds from the gross amount.'],
['What does an agreed financial metric definition supply?','Scope and recognition rules','Guaranteed profit growth','Automatic source accuracy','Universal tax advice','Definitions state what states, amounts, periods and adjustments count for the organization’s purpose.'],
['A shop has net revenue 80 and cost 50. What is gross margin?','37.5%','60%','62.5%','30%','Profit is thirty and margin is 30/80=0.375; 30/50 would be markup.'],
['A team labels unpaid invoices as collected cash. What is confused?','Workflow/accounting state','Only decimal precision','Only currency formatting','Only customer-id length','An invoice can exist before payment; cash collection and invoicing are different events.'],
['A company subtracts refunds twice after merging adjustments. What check helps?','Reconciliation by adjustment identity','Only chart inspection','Only sorting by customer','Only the largest amount','Stable transaction references and reconciliation can expose duplicated adjustment effects.'],
['A report mixes USD and EUR amounts without conversion. What is required?','A reviewed currency policy','Only more decimal places','Only a shared date label','Only an integer total','Different currencies cannot be meaningfully summed without an explicit conversion and valuation convention.'],
['A product has negative net revenue after returns. What should a margin report explain?','The ratio’s business interpretation','That all decimals are invalid','That cost must be zero','That profit is guaranteed positive','Negative or zero denominators can make familiar percentage interpretations misleading and need clear reporting policy.'],
['A dashboard uses recognized revenue while another uses orders placed. Must their totals match?','No, they measure different concepts','Yes for every business','Only when both use SQL','Only when amounts are positive','Recognition rules and order-state timing can produce legitimate differences that should be reconciled conceptually.'],
['An analyst changes refund treatment to improve a reported margin. What is essential?','Approved definition governance','Only a new chart title','Only hiding the prior result','Only increased decimal precision','Metric rules should follow authorized business/accounting policy, not a desired performance outcome.']
]},
{id:'dsm-retention',comps:['m2.c1','m2.c2'],title:'Distinguish cohort retention, customer churn and revenue movement',intro:'Retention and churn depend on who was eligible to remain, when they entered and what counts as active. A new-customer surge can hide poor retention if cohorts are not separated.',sections:[['Define the starting population and observation horizon','A simple customer-retention fraction takes a fixed starting cohort and measures the share still meeting the active criterion after a declared horizon. New arrivals after the start do not belong in that cohort’s denominator or retained numerator. Define how pauses, reactivations, duplicate accounts and missing follow-up are treated.','For the same closed cohort, horizon and complementary active/churned states, retention plus churn equals one. Those conditions often fail in dashboard metrics: gross logo churn, net revenue retention and period-end active counts are different concepts. State the actual definitions rather than assuming every reported churn complements every retention measure.'],['Revenue retention is a different unit of analysis','Customer/logo retention counts entities. Gross revenue retention tracks retained recurring revenue without expansion under an agreed convention; net revenue retention also includes expansion/contraction according to policy. A business can lose customers while retained accounts expand spending.','The fixture follows three original accounts and excludes a newly acquired fourth account. It computes logo retention and a stated revenue ratio for that cohort. It is a descriptive calculation, not a complete recurring-revenue accounting model or evidence that the change caused customer behavior.']],problem:'Keep new accounts out of an original cohort’s retention calculation.',steps:['Fix the original account set and baseline revenue.','Measure their later active status and matched revenue.','Exclude a later arrival from the cohort ratio.'],code:'baseline={"a":100,"b":100,"c":100}\nlater={"a":120,"b":80,"new":200}\nretained=set(baseline)&set(later)\nprint(len(retained)/len(baseline))\nprint(sum(later[k] for k in baseline if k in later)/sum(baseline.values()))\nprint("new" in baseline)',expect:'0.6666666666666666\n0.6666666666666666\nFalse',limit:'Accounts absent later are treated as inactive with zero revenue under this fixture’s policy. Real pauses, reactivations, revenue recognition and incomplete follow-up require separate definitions.',mistakes:['Period-end active count can grow even when an old cohort retains poorly.','Customer retention and revenue retention use different units and can diverge.'],takeaways:['Anchor retention to a cohort and horizon.','Only complementary definitions justify retention=1−churn.'],practice:[
['Which accounts belong in an original cohort-retention denominator?','The eligible starting cohort','Every later acquisition','Only the retained accounts','Only the highest-revenue accounts','A fixed cohort denominator represents the original population whose continued activity is being measured.'],
['Do new customers automatically improve an older cohort’s retention?','No','Yes by definition','Only if they pay more','Only if counts are integers','Later entrants are outside the original cohort and should not change its retention fraction.'],
['When is retention exactly one minus churn?','With matching complementary definitions','For every metric named churn','Only with positive revenue','Only for monthly charts','The same cohort, window and exhaustive complementary states are required.'],
['A business loses many small accounts while large accounts expand. What can occur?','Logo and revenue retention diverge','Every retention rate is identical','All churn becomes zero','New accounts join old cohorts','Entity counts and revenue-weighted movement describe different aspects of the customer base.'],
['A dashboard includes new signups in retained customers. What is distorted?','The cohort numerator','Only display order','Only currency precision','Only the account-name type','New entrants are not members of the baseline cohort and cannot count as retained members of it.'],
['Some users have not reached the follow-up horizon. What needs a policy?','Incomplete observation','Only chart height','Only id capitalization','Only file compression','Incomplete follow-up must not be silently equated with full retention or known churn.'],
['A team changes active from login to paid usage. What should be disclosed?','The retention definition change','Only a new legend color','Only a refreshed logo','Only the data export date','Changing the qualifying activity changes the metric’s meaning and its comparability over time.']
],formal:[
['What is logo retention primarily counting?','Customer entities','Only currency','Only page views','Only product units','Logo/customer retention concerns retained accounts or customers under a defined activity criterion.'],
['Can revenue retention exceed customer retention?','Yes, retained spending can expand','No, their units are identical','Only if accounts are duplicated','Only with missing revenue','Revenue amounts weight customers differently and can include expansion under the metric policy.'],
['What fixes the interpretation of a retention percentage?','Cohort, horizon and active rule','Only the chart title','Only the newest customer count','Only a currency symbol','The population and continued-activity definition determine what the ratio measures.'],
['A service starts with 80 accounts and 60 remain active after its horizon. What is cohort retention?','75%','25%','133%','60%','Retained starting-cohort accounts divided by the original eighty gives 60/80=0.75.'],
['A platform adds 100 new users while half its original users leave. What may an active-user total hide?','Poor original-cohort retention','Guaranteed customer satisfaction','Zero churn in every cohort','Identical spending behavior','Acquisition can offset losses in period-end totals while old cohorts retain poorly.'],
['A report compares net revenue retention with one minus logo churn. What needs review?','Different metric units and rules','Only date formatting','Only column width','Only customer-name spelling','Revenue-weighted expansion/contraction and entity churn are not generally complementary quantities.'],
['A paid subscriber pauses under an allowed policy. What should its status depend on?','The documented retention rule','An analyst’s preferred outcome','A random active assignment','The chart’s green threshold','Pauses and reactivations require an explicit activity/state convention rather than ad hoc classification.'],
['A cohort has only ten days observed for a thirty-day metric. What is missing?','Complete follow-up','A defined account id','The ability to count rows','A numerical type','The cohort has not yet reached the declared horizon, so observed status is not a full thirty-day result.'],
['A company merges duplicate accounts before calculating retention. What should be stable?','The entity-resolution policy','Only the output font','Only row sorting','Only chart orientation','Changing identity resolution can change both cohort membership and retained counts and must be governed.'],
['A retention ratio improves after redefining active more loosely. What cannot be claimed without reconciliation?','Comparable behavioral improvement','That the new formula ran','That the definition changed','That more accounts qualify','A broader definition can raise the metric mechanically without better behavior under the old criterion.']
]},
